EXT. THE ROAD EAST - MONTAGE #7#

-- The crest of the road at sunrise, Asher and the jenny black against the light.
-- The Jordan at noon: brown water to his knees, the jenny's ears back.
-- The bare plain at evening. Far off, a walled city's lights coming on, one by one.

EXT. KALLIAS'S COURT - NIGHT

A colonnaded court in the city, lit by torches and more lamps than anyone could count. Couches, cups, flutes, drums, many languages. At the far end, the open shrine of Tyche, the city's Fortune: a crowned goddess in stone above a roaring brazier, smoke rising to the stars.

Asher lies on a couch in a garland and the purple-hemmed tunic, the city's colour all around him.

KALLIAS (50s), Greek, rings on both hands, a wax tablet and stylus at his elbow, fingers the hem of Asher's tunic.

KALLIAS
Purple at the hem, as a lord's son wears it. And a lord's son counts nothing.

KALLIAS
Whatever name you bore at home, here you shall be Eutychos, the fortunate. A name costs nothing. All else, you shall pay for.

ASHER
(laughing)
Then I am fortunate! Fill every cup!

PHILON (30s), loud, already drunk, raises his.

PHILON
Buy me twice, Eutychos! I am the cheapest friend at this table.

Asher throws an arm around Philon's neck.

ASHER
You are more a brother to me than my brother ever was.

PHILON
Then your brother drinks again, and you pay!

The table roars. Asher turns to Kallias.

ASHER
What does my brother owe you?

Kallias turns the tablet toward him: a long column under Philon's name. Asher takes silver from his purse and drops it on the wax. Kallias draws a line through Philon's column and writes the sum under Asher's. Philon kisses him on both cheeks.

Asher looks up at the colonnade, at the lamps, and throws a handful of silver to the servants.

ASHER
More lamps! Light them all!

Servants run with tapers. The court blazes brighter. At Asher's elbow, Kallias writes.

KALLIAS
Your father must hold half the hills, to send forth such a son.

ASHER
Half the hills he held, until I asked for my half.

KALLIAS
And your brother? If evil found you here, would he come for you?

ASHER
I have no father now. No brother.

Kallias writes it on the tablet.

A priest at the brazier holds out a brimming cup. Asher takes it and, laughing, pours the first of it on the coals before the goddess. The flame leaps. The court cheers.

A woman crosses the floor in dark dyed linen, unhurried. Men look and look away.

KALLIAS
A merchant's wife. Her goodman trades in Sidon, and comes home with the new moon. She is not bought. She chooses.

The woman in dark linen, JES (30s), sits down beside Asher.

JES
They say you asked your father for your share while he still breathes.

ASHER
News travels faster than my jenny.

JES
Every man in this court waits for his father to die. You alone had the courage to say it to his face.

Something crosses Asher's face. Then he smiles, and takes the cup she offers.

He shakes silver from his purse for the next jar. It pours across the table in the torchlight, over his purple hem, and Jes laughs.

EXT. KALLIAS'S COURT - DAWN

Grey dawn on the couches. Bodies asleep. A hot wind out of the east lifts the ash from the goddess's brazier. A wine stain spreads across Asher's purple hem. He stirs. Jes's hand lies on his chest.

ASHER
(half asleep)
At home there is a lamp at the gate. He lights it at every dark.

JES
Sleep. Here, no one wakes you.

He sleeps.

EXT. KALLIAS'S COURT - GATE - NIGHT

Another night. The company spills out, Asher among them. A BLIND BEGGAR sits by the gate, his bowl empty, his hand out.

BLIND BEGGAR
A coin for the blind, young master.

ASHER
Ask my father. He gives to any man who asks.

Philon howls. They pass. The beggar's empty hand stays out.

